findings.detail:
class Life (game/actors/placeholder_anims.gd:85-101) declares `breath_deg = 1.5`, `breath_hz = 0.25`, `bounce_m = 0.005`, ..., `stride_m = 0.62`, `move_speed = 6.0`: a second copy of the Look > Motion tunables and move_speed. HumanoidRig builds clips from Life.from_tuning(), but several tests build them from Life.new():
- the done-when's A4 walks and idle (test_m4_done_when.gd:385, :394);
- test_motion_m4's _clip() (:90), used by its slide and strain tests;
- the player-calm test (:364).

The copies agree with tuning_data.gd and tuning/default.json today, and no test ties them together. Re-tune look_motion_stride_m, or ship a preset, and A4 and test_motion_m4 go on measuring the old stride, a walk nobody sees. This also goes against CLAUDE.md's rule that feel numbers are never script constants.

toon.gdshader mixes the albedo toward the ink by the raw bilinear-filtered mask value. At 1 m one mask texel covers about 1.5-2 px, so every line gets a feathered grey edge. The mask's faint values (0.1-0.5) draw as grey smudges: 1-3.3% of every region's samples, 13.8% on the laces.

What Game-boy sees in first person:
- the tape's wraps read as dotted, hairy pencil strokes;
- the mouth is a fuzzy dotted dash and the nose a faint broken scratch;
- the cap's strap slot is ringed by grey specks;
- the vest seams are dashed.

This is J4's 'crisp interior lines' (critical) and J2's 'crisp' face. It is not in any call: F-punk logs speckle counts, not line softness.

ToonMaterials.art() builds the art material non-glossy (`_make(Kind.ART, false)`), and spec_gloss comes from that one per-material flag. So the whole Punk shares one spec_cut of 0.99 on F-punk-fix's smoothed normals. The highlight falls wherever a broad smooth patch faces the half vector:
- the vest's chest: 4,036 px at C2 f109, a 125x103 px pale-mint shape (198,215,205) on teal (61,141,148);
- both vest panels and the tank top at once in the shove wind-up (C4 f163);
- the cap (C4 f85);
- a white slash on the forehead.

The lens shows none in any C1, C2, C4, C6 or C10 frame I viewed. The look bible: 'Glossy regions (shades, leather, studs) get x3'. The concept's only highlights are the gold lens's white streaks. The area stays under the bible's 3%, so this is placement and shape, not size. It decides part of J5 (one small hard highlight; the cap's highlight) and J2 (the shades read as a flat yellow band). P1-toon.md:129 lists per-region gloss as open, but the vest blobs come from F-punk-fix's smoothing (its F4 noted the cap only).
